uages  say yes like I did today but I'm not  gonna persuade them and I think it's  hard to tell kids in a classroom  you should learn this language because  it's good for you  that's what they do in Canada in the  schools you should learn French because  it's the national language or something  that doesn't do it for most people  however if you can do the equivalent of  this teacher and find out what they're  interested in  might be the Beatles songs in the case  of English might be I don't know I mean  me in terms of in case of Japanese it  might be something else for Spanish  maybe Spanish pop songs I don't know but  I think that's the biggest challenge for  anyone that's in the business of  language instruction is like that  teacher in Algeria how do you find  something that people are interested in  and if they are interested they'll learn  and so then the issue of whether it's  necessary or important really doesn't  matter and you actually learn better if  you are interested in the subject  not surprisingly so if Lady Ashton or  Barisan Ashton is interested in proving  her French and if she goes to Exxon  follows her somewhere and goes to school  and gets introduced to the conditional  and the subjunctive and which verbs  takeoff Wow  and which verbs take it she will waste  her time  however if Lady Ashton or Baroness  Ashton is interested in certain kind of  music maybe she likes Jacques she'll has  never well now I'm dating myself I guess  there's more recent singers or if she  likes gardening or cooking or history or  whatever she likes if she could be  introduced to interesting content and a  way to to you know learn from that  through the repetitive listening  initially and and and doing the kinds of  things that I always talked about at  this book I mean she has a chance I  should also add that this teacher from  Japan said that they have these 50  Japanese kids in a classroom and they go  through texts and basically it's like a  code-breaking exercise wo